 How to use these codes
FOREIGN FITS | Product Code Master 6

Product/style: One design gets one code, for example FF-WOM-PTY-01248. For underwear, the MEN/WOM/KID segment identifies the intended group. Different pack configurations (such as 2-pack and 3-pack) get separate product codes. Size and color do not change the product code.
SKU: Each sellable size/color combination gets its own SKU, for example FF-WOM-PTY-01248-BLK-M and FF-WOM-PTY-01248-BLK-L. Use standardized size and color abbreviations.
Barcode: Assign one unique scannable barcode to each SKU. A barcode may encode your internal SKU or be a separate generated value. Do not assume a made-up 13-digit number is a valid registered GTIN/EAN.
Global sequence: FF-MEN-JKT-00001, FF-WOM-TOP-00002, FF-UWR-MEN-BOX-00003. Generate the next number automatically; never allow manual reuse.
Locations: Keep the same product and SKU across Warehouse, Ganga and Yamuna; record available quantity by location. Do not add a branch to the code.
Supplier reference: Store the supplier article/style number separately so purchases can be traced and reordered.
Hierarchy: Department > category > product type > product/style > variant (SKU and barcode) > stock by location.
Minimum product record: product code, name, department, category, product type, brand, supplier, supplier article code, purchase and landed cost, MRP and selling price. Keep size, color, SKU and barcode on the variant; keep quantities in stock-by-location records.
Governance: Maintain this as a fixed lookup list. An admin should approve new codes, avoid duplicate meanings and never rename codes already assigned to products.
